      <h2 style="font-size: 1.85rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-bottom: 1rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
        Theoretical Foundations: Atomic Orbitals &amp; Quantum Physics
      </h2>
      <p>
        The electronic structure of an isolated atom describes the spatial and energetic distribution of its electrons within the electrostatic potential generated by the atomic nucleus. This distribution is dictated by quantum mechanics through the Schrödinger equation, yielding discrete quantum states characterized by four quantum numbers ($n, l, m_l, m_s$).
      </p>

      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        1. The Three Fundamental Principles of Ground-State Orbital Filling
      </h3>
      <ul style="margin: 0.5rem 0 1.5rem 1.5rem;">
        <li><strong>Aufbau Principle (Madelung Rule):</strong> Electrons occupy subshells in order of increasing $(n + l)$ energy values ($1s &lt; 2s &lt; 2p &lt; 3s &lt; 3p &lt; 4s &lt; 3d \dots$). When two subshells have the same $(n + l)$ sum, the orbital with the lower principal quantum number $n$ fills first.</li>
        <li><strong>Pauli Exclusion Principle:</strong> No two electrons in an atom can share the identical set of all four quantum numbers. Since a single spatial orbital is defined by $(n, l, m_l)$, each orbital can hold a maximum of two electrons, which must have antiparallel spins ($m_s = +1/2$ and $-1/2$).</li>
        <li><strong>Hund's Rule of Maximum Multiplicity:</strong> Within a set of degenerate orbitals (orbitals possessing the identical energy, such as the three $p$ orbitals or five $d$ orbitals), electrons occupy each orbital singly with parallel spins before any pairing occurs, thereby minimizing Coulombic electron-electron repulsion.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Worked Benchmark Scenario -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        2. Worked Analytical Scenario: Transition Metal Ionization of Iron (Fe to Fe²⁺)
      </h3>
      <div style="background: #f8fafc; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.25rem; margin: 1.25rem 0;">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0f172a;">Problem Statement:</p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.75rem; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #475569;">
          Write the ground-state electron configuration of neutral iron (Fe, Z = 26) and explain the formation of the ferrous ion (Fe²⁺) using the outer-shell-first ionization rule.
        </p>

        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.4rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0f172a;">Step 1: Neutral Ground State (Fe, Z = 26)</p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.75rem; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #475569; font-family: monospace;">
          Aufbau sequence: 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s² 3d⁶<br />
          Noble Gas Shorthand: [Ar] 3d⁶ 4s²
        </p>

        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.4rem; font-weight: 700; color: #0f172a;">Step 2: Ionization to Fe²⁺ (Removal of 2 Electrons)</p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.75rem; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #475569; font-family: monospace;">
          Common student error: Stripping from 3d to give [Ar] 3d⁴ 4s² (INCORRECT).<br />
          Correct Rule: Electrons are removed from the highest principal quantum shell (n = 4) first.<br />
          Two electrons are lost from 4s: 4s² &rarr; 4s⁰.
        </p>

        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.92rem; color: #059669; font-weight: 700;">
          Final Configuration: Fe²⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁶ (Paramagnetic, 4 unpaired electrons).
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- FAQ Accordion -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
        Frequently Asked Questions: Electron Configurations
      </h3>

      <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.85rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">Why do transition metal cations lose 4s electrons before 3d electrons?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Although the 4s subshell is filled before 3d in neutral atoms according to the Aufbau principle, once the 3d orbitals contain electrons, interelectronic repulsion causes the 3d orbitals to drop lower in energy than 4s. Therefore, during ionization, electrons are always removed from the outermost principal quantum shell (n=4) first.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">What causes Aufbau ground-state anomalies in Chromium and Copper?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Chromium ([Ar] 3d5 4s1) and Copper ([Ar] 3d10 4s1) promote an electron from 4s to 3d because exactly half-filled (d5) and completely filled (d10) subshells possess maximum quantum mechanical exchange energy and spherical electronic symmetry, which overcomes the energetic cost of promotion.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">How does Hund's Rule govern orbital filling?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Hund's Rule of Maximum Multiplicity dictates that when filling degenerate orbitals of equal energy (such as px, py, pz), electrons must occupy each orbital singly with parallel spins (+1/2) before pairing with opposite spins (-1/2) to minimize interelectronic electrostatic repulsion.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">How is effective nuclear charge (Zeff) calculated using Slater's Rules?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Slater's Rules calculate the shielding constant (S) by grouping orbitals into (1s), (2s,2p), (3s,3p), (3d), etc. Electrons in the same group contribute 0.35 each, electrons in the (n-1) shell contribute 0.85 each, and electrons in deeper shells contribute 1.00 each. The effective nuclear charge is then determined by Zeff = Z - S.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">What is the difference between paramagnetic and diamagnetic species?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            A species is paramagnetic if it possesses one or more unpaired electrons, creating a net magnetic dipole moment that is weakly attracted into external magnetic fields. A species is diamagnetic if all electrons are completely paired in opposite spins, causing it to be weakly repelled by magnetic fields.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

